"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[59049],{59049:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n##  OS file watch limit reached\\nWhen running `yarn run dev` (or `npm run dev`), you may encounter the following error.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"OS file watch limit reached error\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ yarn run dev\\n...\\nCaused by: - OS file watch limit reached\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### Cause\\nThis error occurs when the Linux kernel\'s <mark>inotify</mark> file watch limit is exceeded.\\n\\nDevelopment servers like Next.js, Vite, and Webpack simultaneously watch a large number of files in your project to detect changes and perform <mark>Hot Reload</mark>.  \\nIf the default value of `fs.inotify.max_user_watches` is too low, the number of files to watch exceeds the limit and the error occurs.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Check current inotify watch limit\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ cat /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_watches\\n8192\\n```\\n### Solution\\nYou can resolve this by increasing the `fs.inotify.max_user_watches` value using `sysctl`.\\n\\nFirst, apply it immediately to the current session.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Apply immediately (current session)\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo sysctl fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288\\n$ sudo sysctl -p\\n```\\nAdd a permanent setting to `/etc/sysctl.conf` to persist the change across reboots.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Persist across reboots\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ echo fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288 | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf\\n$ sudo sysctl -p\\n```\\n"}')}}]);